5. # INTELLIGENT TIMETABLE SCHEDULING ENGINE

This is the most important component of the entire system.

Implement the scheduling engine in a dedicated module named scheduler.py using Google OR-Tools CP-SAT.

Do not use random allocation or simple sequential assignment as the primary scheduling method.

The solver must model scheduling decisions as optimization variables and enforce university scheduling constraints.

A. HARD CONSTRAINTS

These constraints must never be violated in a successfully generated timetable:

1. LECTURER CONFLICT PREVENTION A lecturer must not teach two different classes simultaneously.
2. CLASSROOM CONFLICT PREVENTION A classroom must not be assigned to two simultaneous sessions.
3. STUDENT GROUP CONFLICT PREVENTION A student group must not have two compulsory classes simultaneously.
4. ROOM CAPACITY The classroom must accommodate the expected number of students.
5. ROOM TYPE Laboratory sessions must use appropriate laboratories. Other specialized sessions must use suitable facilities.
6. LECTURER AVAILABILITY Classes must be scheduled only during a lecturer's available periods.
7. ROOM AVAILABILITY Classes must be assigned only to available classrooms.
8. WORKING HOURS Classes must fit within the university's permitted working hours.
9. COURSE CONTACT HOURS Every course must receive the required number of weekly teaching hours or sessions.
10. SESSION DURATION Each session must occupy the required number of consecutive time slots.
11. COURSE ASSIGNMENTS Only appropriately assigned lecturers may teach a course.
12. FIXED SESSIONS Approved fixed sessions must remain in their designated periods unless an authorized administrator changes them.
13. GROUP REGISTRATION Prevent conflicts between courses attended by the same student group. Where individual student registrations are available, support conflict checking at the individual student level when required.
14. RESOURCE AVAILABILITY Do not allocate a room or other exclusive resource outside its available periods.
15. VALID TIME INTERVALS Sessions must fit entirely within permitted scheduling periods without overlapping breaks or prohibited intervals.

B. SOFT CONSTRAINTS

Optimize these preferences whenever possible:

- Minimize unnecessary gaps in student timetables.
- Minimize unnecessary gaps in lecturer timetables.
- Distribute sessions fairly across the working week.
- Avoid excessive consecutive teaching hours.
- Balance lecturer workloads.
- Prefer suitable rooms close to the relevant department when location information is available.
- Maximize appropriate classroom utilization.
- Avoid scheduling too many demanding sessions on the same day.
- Respect preferred teaching times.
- Minimize unnecessary changes to previously published timetables.
- Avoid early or late sessions when these are designated as preferences rather than mandatory rules.

Use weighted optimization objectives and configurable priorities.

Never weaken a hard constraint merely to improve a soft constraint.

C. SOLVER BEHAVIOUR

The scheduling engine must:

1. Read and validate all required university data.
2. Detect impossible or contradictory scheduling requirements.
3. Generate candidate time slots and eligible rooms.
4. Create optimization variables.
5. Apply all hard constraints.
6. Define and optimize soft constraints.
7. Run the CP-SAT solver with configurable time limits.
8. Return the best valid solution found.
9. Distinguish between optimal, feasible, infeasible, and unknown solver outcomes.
10. Save the generated timetable only after validating the solution.
11. Explain why a timetable could not be generated when the constraints are infeasible.
12. Preserve the previously published timetable if a new generation attempt fails.

Do not report an optimal solution unless the solver proves optimality.

When the solver finds a feasible solution but cannot prove optimality, report that distinction clearly.

Provide useful diagnostics identifying potential causes of infeasibility, such as insufficient rooms, unavailable lecturers, excessive course hours, or conflicting fixed sessions.

